  {
    "id": "inventory-rc-01",
    "isInventory": true,
    "title": "Epistemological Dilemmas in Algorithmic Governance",
    "genre": "Sociology & Epistemology",
    "difficulty": "IPMAT Advanced / CAT Standard",
    "passage": "In contemporary technological discourse, computational systems are frequently framed as impartial arbiters of complex human activities, purportedly insulated from the visceral emotional biases, ideological doctrines, and cognitive shortcuts that pollute human decision-making. Proponents of digital transformation champion algorithmic governance as an objective, data-driven panacea capable of rationalizing resource distribution, sentencing criminal defendants, and managing economic markets. However, this triumphalist narrative relies on a profound epistemological misconception regarding the ontological nature of software systems: an algorithm is not a neutral mathematical oracle, but a static, codified representation of historical training data.\n\nBecause historical datasets are intrinsically shaped by structural inequities, institutional blind spots, and systemic marginalization, training predictive machine learning models on past human behavior inevitably codifies and amplifies those same socio-historical disparities under the deceptive guise of statistical objectivity. Consider predictive policing software designed to optimize law enforcement resource allocation. When these systems are trained on historical arrest records—data that reflects decades of discriminatory policing practices rather than true criminal activity—they do not predict actual future crime rates. Instead, they mathematically predict where police forces were historically deployed, generating a self-fulfilling feedback loop of hyper-surveillance in historically disenfranchised neighborhoods. The model's statistical 'objectivity' merely cloaks systemic racial and class prejudice in the prestige of mathematical authority.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "id": "q1",
        "question": "Which of the following best captures the primary thesis of the author regarding algorithmic governance?",
        "options": [
          "A) Machine learning algorithms are inherently superior to human decision-making due to their capacity to process multi-dimensional historical datasets without emotional bias.",
          "B) Delegating high-stakes decisions to algorithms mechanizes historical prejudice while shielding systemic bias behind a false veneer of mathematical neutrality.",
          "C) The primary danger of artificial intelligence lies in technical coding flaws that can be fully rectified through rigorous post-deployment auditing.",
          "D) Predictive policing models should be completely abandoned and replaced with classical rule-based expert systems in law enforcement."
        ],
        "answer": 1,
        "explanation": "<strong>🧠 Logical Framework:</strong> The author's central thesis is that algorithms are not neutral; they copy past human bias and cloak systemic prejudice behind mathematical authority."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "inventory-rc-02",
    "isInventory": true,
    "title": "Behavioral Economics and the Paradox of Choice",
    "genre": "Economics & Psychology",
    "difficulty": "IPMAT Advanced / CAT Standard",
    "passage": "In classical economic theory, human beings are modeled as rational actors—often termed 'Homo Economicus'—who possess unlimited cognitive capacity and well-defined preferences. A core axiom of this framework is that expanding the set of available options directly increases consumer welfare. According to standard utility theory, additional choices can never decrease a rational agent's utility because an individual can simply ignore undesirable options and select the single alternative that maximizes their satisfaction. However, contemporary research in behavioral economics has systematically dismantled this foundational dogma, revealing that modern digital consumer environments, characterized by an unprecedented explosion of choices, frequently induce psychological paralysis, heightened regret, and diminished overall satisfaction—a phenomenon famously termed 'the paradox of choice'.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "id": "q1",
        "question": "Which of the following best expresses the author's primary argument in the passage?",
        "options": [
          "A) Classical economic models accurately explain why digital consumer platforms must continuously expand their product catalogs to maximize revenue.",
          "B) Unfettered choice in digital environments burdens consumer cognition, leading to decision paralysis, regret, and reduced satisfaction.",
          "C) Algorithmic recommendation engines have completely solved choice overload by eliminating all unnecessary product options for online shoppers.",
          "D) Consumers should entirely abandon digital e-commerce platforms and return to traditional physical brick-and-mortar stores."
        ],
        "answer": 1,
        "explanation": "<strong>🧠 Logical Framework:</strong> The passage contrasts classical utility theory with behavioral reality, demonstrating that infinite choice overloads human cognition."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
