    "genre": "Computer Science & Cryptography",
    "difficulty": "IPMAT Advanced / CAT Standard",
    "passage": "For nearly half a century, global digital commerce, state intelligence communications, financial banking networks, and military defense infrastructures have rested upon a single mathematical bedrock: asymmetric public-key cryptography. Encryption algorithms such as RSA secure trillions of daily digital transactions by exploiting a fundamental asymmetry in classical computational mathematics. Multiplying two extremely large prime numbers together is computationally trivial for a standard digital processor; however, factoring the resulting massive integer back into its constituent primes requires astronomical computational time. However, the advent of quantum computing threatens to demolish this digital security paradigm via Shor's algorithm.",
